{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"PHASE 1 COMPLETE (rebar-debug) — root causes proven at runtime for all three modes.\n\n## Mode 1 — lease loss -> red CI. CONFIRMED.\n`abort_check` raises `advisory.ReconcileLockLost`, whose MRO is\n`ReconcileLockLost -> RuntimeError -> Exception`. It is NOT `applier.RescheduleError`,\nso `run_pass`'s except arm (`__main__.py:367-375`) falls past the reschedule branch to\n`return 1`. The workflow whitelists only 0/75/3, so exit 1 hits `*)` -> red.\n\nRuntime proof (re-verified independently, not taken on trust):\n    issubclass(ReconcileLockLost, RescheduleError) -> False\n    run_pass(...) -> 1   with stderr byte-identical in shape to the CI log\n\nThe asymmetry is the defect: the SAME event detected BEFORE the pass returns 3 and the\nworkflow calls it \"benign, skipping\". Aborting mid-pass is correct and deliberate\n(ADR 0031; ticket 2711 \"pass aborts, no double-run\") — no exit code was ever specified\nfor it. ADR 0031's own wording decides it: \"a re-run is idempotent\" = EXIT_RESCHEDULE (75),\nalready wired in the workflow as \"next scheduled run will retry\".\nLegitimacy: incomplete-implementation (design authority cited) -> unlocks repair.\n\n## Mode 3 — unhandled 404 aborts the whole pass. CONFIRMED, and the worst of the three.\n`handle_update` soft-fails a 404; `handle_create` and `handle_delete` call their leaves\nwith NO try/except. `applier._apply_one` re-raises `HTTPError` ABOVE its per-mutation\nbackstop on the explicit assumption that \"404 soft-failed in the handler\" — true for 1 of 3\nhandlers. Injecting HTTPError(404) into each leaf:\n    create : ESCAPED (pass-fatal)\n    update : ISOLATED (stale-binding-404)\n    delete : ESCAPED (pass-fatal)\nProduction impact, run 30465914822: applied 1 of 30 planned mutations, **29 valid mutations\nsilently skipped**. That is a direct violation of the acceptance criteria of CLOSED bug\ne534-5154-2401-40fb: \"no valid mutation is silently skipped\".\nLegitimacy: defect — an incompletely-applied prior fix. The general rule was already\nwritten down in `apply_handlers.py` (\"A 404 on a single mutation's target means the issue is\ngone: this is a PER-MUTATION failure, never pass-fatal\") and then applied to one handler.\n\n## Mode 2 — push exhausts 5 retries. CONFIRMED mechanism, deferred fix (see below).\nRecovered the full retry timeline from run 30585738860: all five merges SUCCEEDED and\nintegrated five distinct remote SHAs — the loop converges, then loses the push race each\ntime. Competitor arrival ~8.4s mean; backoff sleeps 3/6/10/17s, so each remote advance lands\nINSIDE the corresponding sleep. Modelled P(exhaust all 5) ~= 12% per run under that burst\nrate, vs ~1.7e-6 with no sleep. Exponential backoff is anti-adaptive here: after a successful\nmerge our HEAD is already fast-forwardable, so sleeping can only forfeit the race it is\nmeant to win. The workflow claims at :319 to \"mirror the local client path (_store/push.py)\"\n— which has NO backoff and re-pushes immediately. It also computes a 5th merge and then\ndiscards it unpushed (`:321-332`), throwing away a free ~93%-likely attempt.\n\n## Additional CONFIRMED latent defect (not the reported symptom)\n`_ref_lock._PUSH_REJECT_MARKERS = (\"stale info\", \"rejected\", \"cannot lock ref\")` over-matches.\nI drove `_push_lease_cas` with real git stderr strings:\n    server-side concurrent ref lock  -> CLASSIFIED AS CAS MISMATCH (\"lease lost/stolen\")\n    remote internal rejection        -> CLASSIFIED AS CAS MISMATCH\n    pre-receive hook declined        -> CLASSIFIED AS CAS MISMATCH\n    GitHub secondary rate limit      -> CLASSIFIED AS CAS MISMATCH\n    auth failed / host unresolved    -> fail-closed (correct)\nThis violates the module's own documented promise (`_ref_lock.py:719-722`): \"a genuine\ntransport failure ... we do NOT classify as a CAS mismatch (fail-closed)\". Worse, that branch\nraises WITHOUT logging the stderr, so a production occurrence cannot be diagnosed after the\nfact — which is exactly why we cannot currently tell whether the two mode-1 lease losses were\ngenuine takeovers or spurious. Both failures landed ~86s in, on the second heartbeat tick\n(lease 120s, interval 40s), which is suspicious but unproven either way.\n\n## Scope of the fix now in flight\nFixing modes 1 and 3 (both Python, both deterministically testable, both with cited\nauthority). RED oracles were written FIRST and confirmed failing for the right reason, then\nheld out from the implementing agent.\nModes 2 and the marker over-match are being filed as linked follow-ups rather than bundled:\nmode 2 changes retry semantics of a shared push path and needs a contention harness, and the\nmarker narrowing changes lock classification on an occurrence that is (by the logging gap\nabove) currently unprovable. Sequencing them is deliberate, not dropping them — the full\nquantified analysis goes on the follow-up tickets."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785453283167118001,"uuid":"ca869bb9-3e6e-428f-abb1-854b0c9a9d94"}